BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 608.0 Td (  In ancient times, the River flowed through a parched valley, holding precious water. Each seas) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 598.0 Td (    on, the River faced a choice: release water to the Sky, or hoard it in deep pools.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 588.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 578.0 Td (  The Sky would multiply whatever the River offered-three drops became nine, one cup became thre) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 568.0 Td (    e. Yet the River feared: "What if the Sky keeps everything? I will run dry.") Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 558.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 548.0 Td (  But an old stone whispered wisdom: "The Sky cannot hold water forever. What rises must fall.") Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 538.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 528.0 Td (  So the River released a measure of water upward. The Sky received it, multiplied it threefold,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 518.0 Td (     and faced its own choice. It could scatter the bounty elsewhere, or return it to the River ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 508.0 Td (    below.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 498.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 488.0 Td (  The wise Sky understood: if it kept everything, the River would dry up and send nothing more. ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 478.0 Td (    If it returned generously, the cycle would continue-both would flourish.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 468.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 458.0 Td (  When the Sky returned the water as rain, the River grew stronger. In the next season, the Rive) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 448.0 Td (    r sent more, trusting the pattern. The Sky returned more still.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 438.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
