BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 198.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 188.0 Td (  In the beginning, the River flowed alone through the valley, keeping all its waters to itself.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 178.0 Td (     The Rain would fall from the sky, offering droplets to the River's course, but the River wo) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 168.0 Td (    uld hoard every bit, never releasing moisture back to the clouds.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 158.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 148.0 Td (  One season, the Rain grew weary and said, "Why should I give when nothing returns?" The Rain w) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 138.0 Td (    ithheld its gift, and the River began to dry.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 128.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 118.0 Td (  An old turtle, keeper of balance, spoke: "Rain, when you give to the River, your gift multipli) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 108.0 Td (    es threefold as it flows through the land, nourishing all below. River, when you return mois) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 98.0 Td (    ture to the sky through mist and vapor, you ensure the Rain can give again.") Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 88.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 78.0 Td (  The Rain understood and sent down generous waters. The River, grateful for this trust, release) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 68.0 Td (    d clouds of mist skyward. The cycle strengthened-each giving created abundance, each return ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 58.0 Td (    enabled more giving.) Tj ET
